{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"\"C:\\\\Program Files\\\\PowerShell\\\\7\\\\pwsh.exe\" -Command 'git show 381e77d4080e30c3449809cd86de076bb3bcb051:docs/plans/WI-961/plan.md'","aggregated_output":"# WI-961 implementation plan, revision 1: Claude transcripts are recorded once per invocation in the usage record\n\nSpec: `docs/work/active/wi-961/WI-961-subagent-usage-ingest.md` (specref: the\nreview-churn README, item 8, proposal steps 2 and 3, under owner ruling 8).\nItem SR: SR-222. Planner: a fresh session that only read. Lane worktree:\n`C:/Projects/ai-template.wt/wi-961` (tip `1d6484cc5`).\n\n## What the code and the box hold today (read, not assumed)\n\n**The code.**\n\n- `session_adapters.py` holds the IF-245 surface. It imports no kit sibling\n  and measures 448 SLOC (`check_complexity.module_sloc`). The one usage\n  builder is `usage_record` (LLR-268): inclusive input, derived fresh input,\n  `\"\"` for an unreported count. `ClaudeAdapter.provider` is `anthropic`.\n- `session_service.record(outcome, meta, report=)` writes one log from the\n  caller's row, overlaid by `outcome.metrics`. Its transcript section is\n  `outcome.stream`. Where the log goes is derived from the root:\n  - a claimed lane: the log is committed there;\n  - any other root: it goes to the primary checkout's `out/sessions/spool/`\n    (`spool_dir`) and nothing is committed.\n- `CALL_LOG_RE` (`^call_[0-9a-f]{32}-\\d{8}-\\d{6}\\.log$`) and `is_call_log`\n  define the standard call-log name. WI-960's `codex_call.py` discounts a\n  commit that only adds such logs, so a derived invocation id must be 32 hex\n  digits or a lane ingest would read as an edit.\n- **`session_service.py` measures 999 SLOC.** `tests/test_module_size_ratchet.py`\n  `THRESHOLD` is 1000. This plan therefore adds no code to it.\n- `agent_common.write_session_log` writes a fixed tuple of header keys.\n  - It names the file `<session>-<stamp>.log` and writes it with `write_text`,\n    so a log of the same name is silently overwritten.\n  - `agent_common.py` sits at its ratchet baseline, 1110.\n  - `log_meta_text` reads a header and reports whether the `# ---` terminator\n    was reached.\n  - `phase_draw_ordinal` skips `call_` names.\n- The gate readers:\n  - `kitlib.verdict.SESSION_LOG_RE` never matches a `call_` name;\n  - `agent_brief.last_build_family` reads `# phase:` and takes only `BUILD`\n    or empty;\n  - `_call_row` writes the role into `phase`.\n- TC-265's structural guard (`tests/test_session_service.py`):\n  - only `session_service.py` calls `write_session_log`;\n  - only `agent_common.py` holds the header literal `agent session log`;\n  - no function that names `iteration` in a string constant calls `write`,\n    `write_text`, `write_bytes` or `open(..., \"w\"/\"a\")`.\n- `usage-source` takes `reported` or `unknown`, and `usage-scope` takes\n  `invocation`, `thread` or `unknown`. No script reads either column.\n\n**The box's transcripts.** The planner surveyed them read-only on\n2026-10-22, across every ambient project directory, from Claude Code\n2.1.2xx to 2.1.30x. No model call was made.\n\n- **Record types.** Main transcripts in this repository's slugs carry 20\n  record types: `queue-operation`, `user`, `attachment`,\n  `file-history-snapshot`, `atis-latch`, `last-prompt`, `ai-title`,\n  `assistant`, `file-history-delta`, `system`, `cost-state`, `mode`,\n  `bridge-session`, `api-request-shape`, `api-request-blob`, `api-request`,\n  `artifact-comment-monitor`, `artifact-autoreact-ledger`, `frame-link` and\n  `custom-title`. Sub-agent transcripts carry 6: `user`, `attachment`,\n  `assistant`, `api-request-shape`, `api-request-blob` and `api-request`.\n- **Assistant records.** There are 67,928 in main transcripts and 118,133 in\n  sub-agent transcripts. Every one has:\n  - `message.id`, `message.model`, an ISO-Z `timestamp`, and integer\n    `input_tokens`, `cache_read_input_tokens`,\n    `cache_creation_input_tokens` and `output_tokens`;\n  - in a main transcript, `isSidechain: false` and no `agentId`;\n  - in a sub-agent transcript, `isSidechain: true` and an `agentId` equal to\n    its file's.\n  - No record carries a `sessionId` other than its own session's: 201 main\n    files and 1,069 sub-agent files were checked.\n- **Only `assistant` records are usage sources.** `usage` also appears in 172\n  `user` records (the parent's tool-result summaries, at\n  `toolUseResult.usage`) and 247 `attachment` records (at\n  `attachment.usage`). Neither is ever read.\n- **Repeated records do not repeat the same usage.** In this repository's\n  slugs, 47,252 records repeat an earlier message id. 20,370 of them differ\n  from it, and only in `output_tokens`. Within a message, `output_tokens`\n  never decreases, and the last record in file order carries the maximum\n  (45,306 messages, no exception). So \"once per message id\" must take each\n  message's **last** record. The first record under-counts sub-agent output:\n  leg 3's builders read 65,107 output tokens by the first record against\n  564,463 by the last.\n- **Two outside checks confirm the last-record rule.**\n  - Nine adjudications ran ambient and were recorded by the service (for\n    example `call_0dca566165df4b72bc02e5c1a6c2b46a`, whose result reads\n    30,039 out, 46 in, 4,012,583 cache read and 181,244 cache write). Each\n    result's `usage` equals, exactly, the last-record sum over its main\n    transcript's unique message ids. Their `modelUsage` also lists a\n    background haiku model (18 to 383 output tokens) that no transcript\n    holds.\n  - The plan's leg 3 is session `4a3bd3cb-802b-4914-bf20-2ea3b5f2ad23`\n    (2026-10-11: nine `kit-builder` agents and one `Plan`). Under the\n    last-record rule it reproduces the plan's figures exactly:\n    - the coordinator: 156 messages, 151,014 output and 33,486,394 cache\n      read;\n    - the builders: 585 messages and 564,463 output;\n    - Plan: 47 messages and 51,313 output.\n- **Metas.** There are 560 in this repository's slugs.\n  - Every one carries `agentType`, `description`, `toolUseId`, `spawnDepth`,\n    `requestShape` and `requestNonInteractive`. 314 also carry `model`.\n  - `spawnDepth` is 1 for 538 and 2 for 22. `parentAgentId` appears exactly\n    on the 22 at depth 2, and every one of them resolves.\n- **Shapes outside the spec's layout exist on this box**, in another\n  project:\n  - one sub-agent transcript with no meta;\n  - a `subagents/workflows/wf_*/` directory of Workflow-tool agents, whose\n    metas lack `toolUseId`, beside a `journal.jsonl`;\n  - one session id held in two project directories (a desktop scratch\n    session whose directory moved; the later file extends the earlier).\n- **Fixture candidates for D2.**\n  - Session `d628449b…`: its depth-1 agent `a1e796bd…` has exactly two\n    depth-2 children.\n  - Sessions `5b1f7546…` and `9c7d73b2…` are alternatives.\n- **The environment.** `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID` is set in a sub-agent's\n  shell to its parent session's id: it was checked in this planner's own\n  shell. `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` is unset, so the ambient home is `~/.claude`.\n\n## Decisions this plan takes (for the coordinator to confirm at the claim's records)\n\n1. **Where the code goes.**\n   - **The reader** goes in `session_adapters` (Deliverable 1). It is the\n     only code that knows the transcript shape: module functions beside the\n     claude adapter, still importing no kit sibling.\n   - **The command** is a new shipped module,\n     `project-trajectory/scripts/usage_ingest.py`. It holds:\n     - the exactly-once read;\n     - the double-count skip;\n     - the composition of each log;\n     - the call to `session_service.record`.\n\n     It is not placed in `session_service`, which sits at 999 SLOC against\n     the 1000 threshold. A command is its own CLI anyway, like\n     `coordinator_adjudicate.py`.\n   - **The new header columns** go in `agent_common.write_session_log`.\n2. **The counting rule.** Each message id is counted once, from its last\n   record in file order. Messages are ordered by first appearance.\n   - Usage is read only from `assistant` records' `message.usage`.\n   - `transcript_usage(messages)` maps a run of messages to the usage record\n     through `usage_record`, LLR-268's one builder:\n     - `fresh` is the sum of `input_tokens`;\n     - cache read and cache write are the sums of `cache_read_input_tokens`\n       and `cache_creation_input_tokens`;\n     - output is the sum of `output_tokens`;\n     - reasoning is the sum of `output_tokens_details.thinking_tokens` where\n       reported, `\"\"` where none is;\n     - `cli` is `claude` and the provider name `anthropic`.\n   - The model is that of the last message whose model is not `<synthetic>`.\n3. **Identity.**\n   - An invocation is one transcript: the session's own (agent id `\"\"`,\n     depth 0, agent type `\"\"`), or one sub-agent's (its agent id, its meta's\n     `spawnDepth`, `agentType` and `parentAgentId`).\n   - `invocation-id` is `uuid.uuid5(<a fixed namespace constant in the\n     command>, \"<session id>/<agent id>\").hex`: 32 hex digits, deterministic\n     and never `uuid4`. `is_call_log` accepts the resulting name.\n   - The role is `COORDINATOR` for the session's own transcript and\n     `SUBAGENT` for a sub-agent at any depth. `phase` takes the same value,\n     as `_call_row` does.\n4. **The contribution.** A log records one contribution:\n   - `message-count`, the number of unique message ids it counted;\n   - `message-cursor`, the last message id counted;\n   - `contribution-key`, `<invocation-id>:<first message id>:<last message\n     id>`, immutable once written;\n   - `started-at` and `ended-at`, the first and last message timestamps;\n   - `stamp` and `date`, from the last message's time in the local form\n     `_call_row` uses.\n\n   Two contributions of one invocation therefore get distinct names\n   whenever their last messages fall in different seconds. If a log of the\n   exact name already exists, the command refuses that invocation and names\n   the log; it never overwrites one.\n5. **What exactly-once reads.**\n   - **Which logs.** It reads the logs already recorded for this invocation,\n     found by **name** (`call_<invocation-id>-*.log`, so no header is read\n     to classify relevance) in:\n     - the primary checkout's spool (`session_service.spool_dir(root)`);\n     - the root's own `docs/iteration/`;\n     - the `docs/iteration/` of every worktree `git worktree list` names\n       (`agent_common.worktree_records`).\n\n     Logs are de-duplicated by file name.\n   - **The cursor.** Each log's header is read with\n     `agent_common.log_meta_text`. The new contribution is the messages\n     after the furthest recorded cursor in the transcript's order.\n   - **Refusals.** The command refuses that invocation (exit 1, naming the\n     log) when:\n     - a header is unterminated or unreadable;\n     - a header lacks `message-cursor` or `contribution-key`;\n     - a cursor is not in the transcript.\n\n     The reason: re-counting or skipping would break exactly-once (Trust).\n   - **Nothing new to record.** No message after the cursor records nothing\n     and prints `unchanged`.\n   - **Where the function lives.** The read sits in its own function, which\n     holds no `write` call (the TC-265 guard).\n6. **No double count (D5).**\n   - A session is service-recorded when any log in the set above (all names,\n     header read) carries its `session-id` with a `usage-source` other than\n     `transcript`. Its main transcript is then skipped and named.\n   - Its sub-agents are skipped when, and only when,\n     `session_adapters.CLAUDE_RESULT_COUNTS_SUBAGENTS` is true. That constant\n     is set from P0's probe B, and its docstring cites the decision id.\n   - A log in that set whose header cannot be read refuses what the skip\n     governs: the main transcript, and the sub-agents too when the constant\n     is true. Such a log may be the service's record of this session.\n7. **The closed shape (S3).** `TRANSCRIPT_TYPES` is the allow-set of record\n   types, fixed from P0's survey.\n   - A record must be one JSON object whose `type` is in the set.\n   - An `assistant` record also needs:\n     - `message.id`, `message.model` and `timestamp` as strings;\n     - `message.usage` with the four integer counts;\n     - `sessionId` equal to the session;\n     - in a main transcript, `isSidechain` false and no `agentId`;\n     - in a sub-agent's, `isSidechain` true and `agentId` equal to its file's.\n   - A meta needs `agentType` (a string) and `spawnDepth` (an integer of 1 or\n     more, not a bool). It needs `parentAgentId` when `spawnDepth` is 2 or\n     more. Other meta keys are ignored.\n   - Any value of `spawnDepth` is accepted and recorded. A parent that names\n     no agent in the directory is recorded as written and printed as\n     `parent unresolved`.\n   - Each of these is refused **per transcript**, naming the file and the\n     field: a torn or non-JSON line, a missing meta, and any entry under\n     `subagents/` other than an `agent-<id>.jsonl` and `agent-<id>.meta.json`\n     pair (the Workflow layout included). Nothing is recorded for that\n     transcript; the others are recorded, and the exit is 1.\n8. **The home and the session.**\n   - The home is `CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR` when the command's environment sets it,\n     else `~/.claude`: the home Claude Code itself uses there. The command\n     never selects an account home, per LLR-336 and the spec's bound.\n   - The session is `--session ID`, else `CLAUDE_CODE_SESSION_ID`. With\n     neither, the command exits 2.\n   - It is located as `<home>/projects/*/<id>.jsonl` and\n     `<home>/projects/*/<id>/`:\n     - two project directories holding it: refused, both paths named;\n     - neither: named absent, exit 1;\n     - a missing main transcript beside a present `subagents/`: the main is\n       named absent and the sub-agents are read.\n9. **What a log holds.** The row the command composes carries:\n   - `session` `call_<invocation-id>`;\n   - `phase` and `role`;\n   - `outcome` `INGESTED`;\n   - `model`, the response model;\n   - `gen_ai.request.model`, the meta's `model` when present, else `\"\"`;\n   - `session-id` and `gen_ai.conversation.id`, the session id;\n   - the usage record from decision 2;\n   - `usage-source` `transcript` and `usage-scope` `contribution`;\n   - `usage-status` `known`: the closed shape (decision 7) guarantees both\n     the input and the output counts, so nothing re-derives\n     `session_service._status` here;\n   - the identity columns (`agent-id`, `parent-agent-id`, `spawn-depth`,\n     `agent-type`);\n   - the contribution columns from decision 4.\n\n   Provider, tier, roster row, cost, wall time, occupancy, train,\n   `session-mode` and `raw-usage` stay `\
